  "rounds": {
    "1": {
      "note": "Cantonese verbs don't change form for past, present, or future — instead, small particles and time words do the work. <strong>zo2</strong>, <strong>gan2</strong>, <strong>gwo3</strong>, and <strong>zyu6</strong> attach <strong>after</strong> the verb (e.g. <em>sik6 zo2</em> = ate, <em>sik6 gan2</em> = eating). Modals like <strong>wui5</strong> (will) and <strong>jiu3</strong> (need to) come <strong>before</strong> the verb. Time words like <em>ji5 ging1</em> (already) and <em>mei6</em> (not yet) sit at the start or before the verb to anchor when something happens.",
      "words": [
        {
          "c": "咗",
          "j": "zo2",
          "e": "(completed action)"
        },
        {
          "c": "緊",
          "j": "gan2",
          "e": "(currently happening)"
        },
        {
          "c": "過",
          "j": "gwo3",
          "e": "(experienced before)"
        },
        {
          "c": "住",
          "j": "zyu6",
          "e": "(ongoing state)"
        },
        {
